OK, sorry for delay...

There are two clocks in this picture (maybe more). One is commonly
referred to as the TOY clock, which is the one which tells VMS what
the date and time is. You've found that one, and it is indeed
interrogatable via software, either via VMS or more primitively by
fiddling with physical addresses. That is *not* the one I was
interested in.

Somewhere on the main logic board there will be a crystal oscillator
which provides the master clock for the processor and memory timing.
If you have the VAXstation 4000 Service Guide EK-KA490-SV it says the
relevant chip is a DC509, and Wikipedia says the CPU clock is
74.43MHz.

The field replaceable unit for faults like this is the system board so
the service guide doesn't go into the level of detail we need here,
which is unfortunate.

There is a picture of a VAXstation 4000 model 90 system board at
http://ummr.altervista.org/digital_equipment_corporation_cp.htm#VAXstation%=
204000/90

It shows two metal-encased things near the big chip (the one with no
heat sink) which are likely to be crystal clock oscillator modules.
One of these is likely to be the system's master clock. It might or
might not be DC509. But I'm guessing that somewhere round here,
something that should oscillate may not be oscillating (or the absence
of DC OK is inhibiting the required activity somewhere else).

In the absence of circuit diagrams or better pictures I'm starting to
be out of my depth now. I have a VAXstation 4000 model 90A here but it
does not match the Model 90 picture above (mine only has one clock
module, which is labelled at 330MHz or something like that).

Not much more I can add just now. At least you've found a way to make
it work, but it's not one I'd be comfortable with medium term.

Once again, best of luck.


