I think (but am not 100% sure) that this supports the "DC OK is bust"
theory. There'll be a capacitor/diode/logic combination somewhere such
that in normal operation DCOK is not asserted until all the PSU
voltages have been within tolerance for a few tens of milliseconds. If
one of those capacitors fails, as electrolytics sometimes do, the
behaviour may no longer be appropriate, but it is conceivable that
some creative use of the on/off switch will make it Do The Right Thing
(at least temporarily). DCOK may be an input to the LED-driving logic,
directly or indirectly. Can't find a circuit diagram (of anybody's DC
OK eg PDP11!) to illustrate it right now, and need to be elsewhere, so
other contributions most welcome.


