I assume all PSU output voltages have been checked at steady-state and
seem OK, in both working and non-working states?

There will also somewhere be a signal equivalent to "DC OK", to
inhibit the logic from starting up before the power outputs are
stable. I do not know exactly what it will be called but I am
reasonably confident it will exist. It may come direct from the PSU or
it may be generated on the motherboard. Do you have enough info, kit,
skill and motivation to find this signal and put a scope on it in
working mode and in non working mode, and (ideally) watch what happens
to it as you power up the box?

Similarly, do you have enough info, kit, skill and motivation to find
the CPU clock and check if it is live?

Given what you report re bouncing the power, I'm wondering if
something has failed to start up right. My first thought in general
would be a fault with the power supply but given that you have swapped
it already, it probably isn't that (especially as this may well pre-
date switched mode power supplies???????) .

My next thought is the logic that generates DC OK largely because this
*ought* to be relatively simple to spot and check and (hopefully) fix.
Same hopefully goes for the CPU clock.

Best of luck.


